# Zero technical debt

Attitude adapted from TigerBeetle
[TigerStyle](https://github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle/blob/main/docs/TIGER_STYLE.md)
zero-debt. Fleet principles:
[docs/standards.md](../../docs/standards.md) (Zero technical debt).

**Order:** correctness and operability, then clarity, then ship speed.

**Ship solid.** Do not merge or hand back work with known defects,
vacuous guards (checks that always pass over empty data), stale
operator docs, or in-scope “follow-up later.” Missing features are fine.
Half-done quality is not.

**Assertions must bite.** A guard that evaluates over `[]` or never runs
in CI is not a guard — replace or delete it.

**No CI validation is debt.** If a class of code can break production and
nothing in GitLab CI fails when it is wrong, that gap is technical debt.
Add or extend a CI check in the same change (or delete the unguarded
path). Local-only scripts and Darwin-skipped binaries are fine only when
CI on the native builder still runs them
([docs/deployments.md](../../docs/deployments.md)).

**ADR freezes are not debt.** Intentional decisions in
[infrastructure.mdc](infrastructure.mdc) and accepted ADRs stay. Do not
confuse them with unfinished quality.

**Review deferrals.** Polite “follow-up OK” does not park in-scope items
unless the operator overrides that turn.

Done bar for edits remains [fmt-lint.mdc](fmt-lint.mdc). This rule adds:
no known leftover in the change.
